WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.650
Don't look now, but writer-director Leigh
Whannell's The Invisible Man is shaping up

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.900
to be one of the year's biggest horror hits.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.940
The flick has been screened for critics, and
while there is still an embargo on formal

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.700
reviews, they're taking to social media in
droves to report that The Invisible Man is

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.900
something you need to see.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.260
It's apparently a tense, terrifying thriller
grounded in real-world issues and featuring

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.700
a performance for the ages from Elisabeth
Moss.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.900
A bit of background: The Invisible Man is
the result of a major course-correction by

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.279
studio Universal, which at one point was planning
a Marvel-esque series of interconnected franchises

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.380
based on its classic monster properties.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.220
The first in the series, 2017's remake of
The Mummy, also ended up becoming the last

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.940
when it was savaged by critics and underperformed
severely at the box office.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.440
Plans for big-budget tentpole features starring
A-list actors based on properties like Bride

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.700
of Frankenstein and The Invisible Man were
subsequently scrapped, and Universal wisely

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.260
decided to scale back its plans.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.180
"Okay, we're just going to take a minute and
come up with a new plan."

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.960
"You don't know what you're doing."

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.340
"I know exactly what I'm doing."

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.440
Universal's new approach is to make modestly-budgeted,
updated takes on its properties crafted by

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.920
experienced horror filmmakers.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.420
To help implement this plan, the studio partnered
with the hitmaking horror studio Blumhouse,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.560
and the new Invisible Man is the first fruit
of that partnership.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.920
The flick may only be Whannell's third feature
as a director, but the writer and co-creator

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.100
of Saw proved long ago that he knows a thing
or two about horror.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.040
His previous movie in the director's seat,
2018's Upgrade, also showed a ton of promise

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.160
- promise upon which he has apparently now
delivered.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.700
Critics are going absolutely bonkers over
The Invisible Man, in no small part due to

00:01:34.709 --> 00:01:37.899
his direction, and to Elisabeth Moss' committed
performance.

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:42.880
Moss stars as Cecilia Kass, whose boyfriend
Adrian was an abusive jerk to her before he

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.860
apparently went off and mysteriously died.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.119
Cecilia eventually becomes convinced that
her deceased former lover has returned to

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:53.209
torment her as an invisible man, although
she understandably has a hard time getting

00:01:53.209 --> 00:01:54.260
anyone to believe her.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.740
Critic Witney Seibold was one of many to praise
the film's examination of the dynamics of

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.880
abuse while also standing in awe of just how
scary it is.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:02.680
He tweeted:

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.719
"Leigh Whannell's #TheInvisibleMan is not
just one of the most tightly-wound thrillers

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:10.709
in recent memory, but it is a pertinent and
all-too-real look at the panic and damage

00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:12.540
left by an abusive relationship.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.180
Elisabeth Moss digs deep."

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.900
We Live Entertainment editor-in-chief Scott
Menzel concurred, tweeting:

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:22.280
"The Invisible Man is [a] tension filled thrill-ride
from start to finish [...] Elisabeth Moss

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.180
delivers a haunting &amp; captivating performance
that is amongst some of her finest work to

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:26.680
date."

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.740
The Playlist's Griffin Schiller called the
film:

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.800
"...a spine-tingling modern horror classic
in the making.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.379
Poignant &amp; timely, packed full of edge of
your seat anxiety inducing suspense!

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.740
Sound &amp; production design are MASSIVE standouts
but Elizabeth Moss OWNS this film."

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:45.500
Virtually all of the critics who took to Twitter
to freak out over The Invisible Man were in

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.310
awe of Whannell's command of his craft, and
his ability to generate nerve-wracking suspense.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.340
Daily Dead News managing editor Heather Wixson
tweeted:

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.960
"Leigh Whannell has crafted a masterclass
in tension and terror, and I think my jaw

00:02:56.969 --> 00:02:59.079
was left on the floor at least twice.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.140
Completely thrilling, visceral and emotional."

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.360
Meanwhile, Fringefeed editor Julian Wright
offered the kind of praise we can really get

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:05.860
behind.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:06.800
He said:

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.380
"I needed to pee the entire film but couldn't
move.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.140
Then got home and was convinced an invisible
entity was standing in the corner watching

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:13.640
me.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:14.740
It's that great."

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.560
Just in case you're not yet convinced, consider
the words of Coming Soon contributor Grant

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.840
Hermanns, whose praise might seem hyperbolic
if we hadn't gotten the chills just from watching

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:24.700
the flick's trailers.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.020
Hermanns tweeted:

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.100
"[The Invisible Man] is one of the best horror
films in years.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.220
Leigh Whannell has set the bar high for the
rest of the year.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.590
It's tense from start to finish in the same
vein as a Hitchcock masterpiece.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:40.129
I seriously can't recommend this movie enough,
I plan to see it multiple times!"

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:44.530
With initial reactions like this, The Invisible
Man could wind up being a major hit - out

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:45.160
of nowhere.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.320
You'll get the chance to see the film for
yourself when it opens on February 28.

